WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.450
Florida’s waterways are full of drawbridges, and they have a little language of their own.

2
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.525
To ask for an opening, sound one prolonged blast followed by one short.

3
00:00:10.775 --> 00:00:13.600
Or just call the bridge tender on the radio.

4
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.975
In Florida, most bridges monitor VHF channel nine.

5
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:24.375
If the bridge is going to open, it answers with the same signal: one prolonged and one short.

6
00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:26.925
And if it can’t open right now?

7
00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:29.875
Five short blasts.

8
00:00:30.125 --> 00:00:31.850
Sound familiar?

9
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.100
It’s the danger signal.

10
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.425
It means, don’t come ahead.

11
00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:41.475
Many bridges only open on a schedule, say on the hour and the half hour.

12
00:00:41.725 --> 00:00:48.000
The schedules are in the federal regulations and in the Coast Pilot, so look them up before you go.

13
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.650
And hold well back until the span is fully open.

14
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.400
A current can carry you into a half-open bridge faster than you’d believe.
